{"id": 22, "ts": 1780634049.3297842, "event": "public_discussion_message", "round": 2, "agent": "james_carter", "message": "I remain convinced that Candidate\u202fC is the strongest overall fit.\u202fSarah and I have already highlighted C\u2019s 100\u202f% reliability, calmness in crises, quick correct decisions, and the positive crew atmosphere he creates\u2014direct evidence of operational reliability, stress resilience, decision quality, and crew cooperation.\u202fWhile C\u2019s egocentric attitude and limited willingness for further education are noted, they have not yet manifested as safety\u2011critical behavior, and his proven teamwork outweighs those concerns.\nIn contrast, Candidate\u202fA, despite impressive abilities to anticipate danger, see complex connections, and demonstrate strong leadership, offers no concrete evidence of operational reliability or crew\u2011cooperation, and is described as unfriendly and a show\u2011off\u2014traits that could undermine long\u2011haul cockpit dynamics.\u202fCandidate\u202fD, although strong on problem\u2011solving and responsibility, is characterized as moody, a loner, and potentially hot\u2011tempered; without clear evidence of how this impacts teamwork, the risk remains too high for a role that demands consistent crew collaboration.\u202fCandidate\u202fB\u2019s technical strengths are offset by documented grumpiness, uncooperative behavior, nasty remarks toward colleagues, and a weak numeric memory that could affect attention to detail.\nGiven the current evidence, Candidate\u202fC best satisfies the critical safety\u2011related criteria for a long\u2011distance pilot."}
